      "text": "To solve the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we first need to find the values of \\(x\\) that satisfy the equation.\n\nStarting with the equation:\n\\[\n(x - 5)^2 = 9\n\\]\nwe take the square root of both sides:\n\\[\nx - 5 = \\pm 3\n\\]\nThis gives us two separate equations to solve:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\n\\]\nSolving these equations, we get:\n\\[\nx - 5 = 3 \\implies x = 8\n\\]\n\\[\nx - 5 = -3 \\implies x = 2\n\\]\nSo, the solutions to the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\).\n\nNext, we need to find the sum of these roots. The roots are 8 and 2. Adding them together, we get:\n\\[\n8 + 2 = 10\n\\]\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
