      "text": "To find the sum of the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\), we should start by solving the equation for \\(x\\).\n\nFirst, we recognize that the given equation is a quadratic equation in terms of \\(x - 5\\). To simplify, let's make the substitution \\(y = x - 5\\). This changes the equation to:\n\\[y^2 = 9\\]\n\nNext, we solve for \\(y\\) by taking the square root of both sides:\n\\[y = \\pm 3\\]\n\nSince \\(y = x - 5\\), we can now find the values of \\(x\\) by substituting \\(y\\) back into the equation:\n\\[x - 5 = 3 \\quad \\text{or} \\quad x - 5 = -3\\]\n\nSolving these two equations separately, we get:\n\\[x = 3 + 5 = 8\\]\n\\[x = -3 + 5 = 2\\]\n\nSo, the roots of the equation \\((x - 5)^2 = 9\\) are \\(x = 8\\) and \\(x = 2\\).\n\nTo find the sum of the roots, we add these two values together:\n\\[8 + 2 = 10\\]\n\nTherefore, the sum of the roots of the equation is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{10}\n\\]",
